    passage: 'Have you ever wondered where rain comes from? The answer lies in something scientists call the water cycle. The water cycle is a process that describes how water moves around our planet. First, the heat from the sun causes water in oceans, lakes, and rivers to turn into water vapour. This process is called evaporation. As the water vapour rises into the sky, it becomes cooler and turns back into tiny drops of water. This step is known as condensation, and these tiny drops form clouds. When the clouds become too heavy, the drops fall back to the ground as rain or snow, which we call precipitation. The water then flows into rivers and oceans, and the cycle starts again. The water cycle is important because it gives us fresh water to drink and helps plants grow. Without it, life on Earth would not be possible. Scientists have studied the water cycle for hundreds of years, but they are still learning new things about how clouds form and how rain begins. Understanding this cycle also helps us predict the weather and prepare for floods or droughts. Next time you see rain, remember that the same water may have fallen on dinosaurs millions of years ago.',
