    title: 'Helen Keller\'s Story',
    passage: 'Helen Keller is one of the most inspiring people in modern history. She was born in 1880 in the United States. When she was only nineteen months old, a serious illness made her lose both her sight and her hearing. For the next few years, Helen lived in a world of silence and darkness. She could not communicate with others easily, and she often became angry and frustrated. Everything changed in 1887 when her teacher, Anne Sullivan, came to her home. Anne was very patient and taught Helen how to connect words with objects by using her fingers. Slowly, Helen learned to read, write, and even speak. She worked extremely hard and later went to university, becoming the first deaf-blind person to earn a college degree. Helen wrote many books, including her famous autobiography "The Story of My Life". She travelled around the world and gave speeches to encourage people with disabilities. She spent her whole life fighting for equal rights and education for the blind and deaf. Helen Keller proved that nothing is impossible if you have courage and determination. Her story still inspires millions of people today to face their own challenges with hope.',
