    category: '历史',
    title: 'The Ancient Silk Road',
    passage: 'More than two thousand years ago, a Chinese official named Zhang Qian opened a long trade route that later became known as the Silk Road. This road was not a single road, but a network of paths that connected China with Central Asia, the Middle East, and even Europe. Merchants travelled along the Silk Road carrying silk, tea, porcelain, and spices from China, and brought back gold, silver, glass, and horses from the West. The journey was extremely dangerous. Traders had to cross vast deserts, climb high mountains, and face cold winds and sandstorms. Because of these dangers, camel caravans were used to carry heavy goods across the dry lands. The Silk Road was more than just a trade route. It was also a road for sharing ideas, languages, religions, and inventions. Buddhism, for example, travelled from India into China along this road. Today, parts of the ancient Silk Road have been turned into popular tourist routes, and people can still visit old cities and markets along the way. In 2013, China introduced the Belt and Road Initiative to build new roads and railways that connect modern countries. The dream of friendly trade and cultural exchange continues to grow.',
