    category: '科学',
    title: 'Why Volcanoes Erupt',
    passage: 'A volcano is a mountain with a hole at the top, and deep inside the Earth, hot melted rock called magma waits to come out. When the pressure becomes too great, the magma pushes up through cracks in the Earth\'s surface and bursts out as lava. This is what we call a volcanic eruption. Some volcanoes erupt quietly, with lava flowing slowly down the sides. Others explode violently, sending ash, rocks, and smoke high into the sky. The temperature of lava can be over one thousand degrees Celsius, hot enough to melt metal. Although volcanoes can be very dangerous, they are also useful. Volcanic soil is rich in minerals, which helps plants grow very well. Many people live near volcanoes because the land is good for farming. Scientists study volcanoes to predict when they might erupt and warn people to leave the area. There are about fifteen hundred active volcanoes on land around the world, and many more under the ocean. One of the most famous volcanoes is Mount Vesuvius in Italy, which destroyed the city of Pompeii in 79 AD. Studying volcanoes helps us understand how the Earth changes over time and how mountains and islands are formed.',
