    title: 'Tu Youyou and the Cure for Malaria',
    passage: 'Tu Youyou is a Chinese scientist who won the Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine in 2015. She became the first Chinese woman to win a Nobel Prize in science. Tu Youyou grew up in the 1950s and studied at Beijing Medical University. After finishing school, she worked at the China Academy of Traditional Chinese Medicine. In the 1960s, malaria was killing thousands of people around the world, especially in Africa and Southeast Asia. Existing medicines were losing their power against the disease. Tu Youyou was given the difficult task of finding a new cure. She looked back at old Chinese medical books and found a plant called sweet wormwood, which had been used for hundreds of years to treat fevers. Tu Youyou and her team tested the plant carefully. After many failures, they finally extracted a substance called artemisinin. This new medicine worked very well against malaria and has since saved millions of lives around the world. Tu Youyou did not patent her discovery, so that the medicine could be made cheaply for everyone. Her success shows that traditional knowledge and modern science can work together. She once said that scientists should be ready to work for the good of all people, not just for fame or money.',
