    title: 'Video Games and Learning',
    passage: 'Video games are very popular among teenagers today. Many parents worry that games are bad for their children, but studies show that some games can actually help the brain. For example, puzzle games teach us how to solve problems step by step. Adventure games often require us to read carefully and remember useful information. Strategy games train us to plan ahead and think about the results of our actions. My favourite game is a building game where I create my own city. I have to balance money, build roads, and keep the people in my city happy. This game has helped me understand basic ideas about economics and city planning. Of course, playing too many games is harmful. If I play for more than two hours a day, I feel tired and cannot focus on my homework. My parents have set a clear rule: I can play games for only one hour on school days and two hours on weekends. After playing, I always go outside to run or play basketball with my friends. I think video games are like many other things in life: in small amounts they can be useful and fun, but in large amounts they can cause problems. Balance is the most important thing, and parents and children should talk about it openly.',
