    title: 'Planting Trees for Tomorrow',
    passage: 'Last spring, our school organised a tree-planting activity on the hill behind our playground. All the students in Grade Seven took part, and we each planted a small sapling. The teachers showed us how to dig a deep hole, place the young tree carefully, and cover the roots with soft soil. After planting, we poured a bucket of water around each tree. My friends and I decided to plant our trees next to each other so we could watch them grow together. We wrote our names on small wooden signs and put them in front of the trees. Now, every time I pass the hill, I look at my tree to see if it has grown taller. Planting trees is one of the best ways to protect our environment. Trees take in carbon dioxide and give out oxygen, which is the air we breathe. They also provide shade in summer and homes for birds and small animals. Sadly, people cut down millions of trees every year, and many forests are getting smaller. If everyone plants at least one tree a year, the world will become greener and cooler. I hope to come back to this hill in ten years and see a small forest of the trees we planted. Taking care of the Earth starts with small actions like these.',
