    category: '文化',
    title: 'The Mid-Autumn Festival',
    passage: 'The Mid-Autumn Festival is one of the most important traditional festivals in China. It falls on the fifteenth day of the eighth lunar month, when the moon is the roundest and brightest of the whole year. The festival celebrates harvest and family reunion. On this evening, family members sit together, admire the bright full moon, and eat mooncakes. Mooncakes are round pastries filled with sweet bean paste, lotus seeds, or even ice cream. They symbolise the full moon and the togetherness of the family. In some places, people also eat pomelos and taro because they are in season. The most beautiful part of the festival is the moon itself. My grandmother used to tell me the story of Chang\'e, the fairy who flew to the moon and lived there with a jade rabbit. When I was small, I liked to imagine that I could see her dancing on the moon. Last year, my family held a small party on our balcony. We brought out a table with tea, fruits, and mooncakes, and we talked and laughed until late at night. The moon that night was so bright and clear that it lit up our whole street. The Mid-Autumn Festival reminds us that no matter how busy we are, we should always spend time with the people we love.',
