    category: '历史',
    title: 'Building the Great Wall',
    passage: 'The Great Wall of China is one of the largest building projects in human history. It was not built in a single dynasty, but constructed and repaired by many different rulers over more than two thousand years. The earliest walls were built during the Spring and Autumn Period, when small states built walls to protect themselves from their enemies. Later, the first emperor of China, Qin Shi Huang, ordered the walls to be joined together to form one long wall in 221 BC. In the Han Dynasty, the wall was extended westward into the desert, and in the Ming Dynasty, it was rebuilt with strong bricks and watchtowers. Millions of soldiers, farmers, and prisoners worked on the wall. They carried heavy stones on their backs and mixed sticky paste from rice and lime to hold the bricks together. Life was extremely hard for the workers, and many died from exhaustion, hunger, or cold weather. Today, the Great Wall stretches more than twenty thousand kilometres and has become a symbol of Chinese wisdom and determination. Although parts of it have been damaged over time, restoration projects are working to keep this wonder alive. Walking on the Great Wall is like walking through thousands of years of history.',
