    category: '名人故事',
    title: 'Edison and the Light Bulb',
    passage: 'Thomas Edison is one of the greatest inventors in history. He was born in 1847 in the United States, and during his lifetime he obtained more than one thousand patents for his inventions. Edison was not a top student at school, but he was very curious about how things worked. His mother taught him at home and encouraged him to read a lot. Edison set up his first laboratory at the age of twenty, and from then on he spent most of his time experimenting. One of his most famous inventions is the practical electric light bulb. Before Edison, other inventors had made light bulbs, but they burned out after only a few minutes. Edison tried more than three thousand different materials before he found one that could glow for a long time without breaking. He also designed a whole system of wires and power stations to bring electricity into people\'s homes. Edison used to say that genius was one percent inspiration and ninety-nine percent perspiration, meaning that hard work was the key to success. He also failed many times but never gave up. Edison\'s inventions, including the phonograph and the movie camera, changed daily life for millions of people. His story still inspires young inventors today to be patient, curious, and brave enough to try again and again.',
