    title: 'Leonardo da Vinci, the Original Renaissance Man',
    passage: 'Leonardo da Vinci was born in Italy in 1452 and is considered one of the greatest minds in human history. He is best known today as the painter of the Mona Lisa and The Last Supper, two of the most famous paintings in the world. However, Leonardo was far more than an artist. He was also an engineer, an inventor, a musician, a scientist, and an architect. In his notebooks, he drew designs for flying machines, armoured vehicles, and even a primitive robot hundreds of years before such things could be built. He studied human bodies by cutting them open in hospitals, which helped him draw the human figure more accurately than any artist before him. He was also deeply interested in nature, water, and light, and he spent long hours watching how things moved. Leonardo never stopped asking questions, and he wrote that learning was a lifelong journey. Although he left many projects unfinished, his ideas have inspired scientists and artists for over five hundred years. He is often called "the original Renaissance man" because he could do so many different things so well.',
