    level: 2,
    serial: 37,
    category: '文化',
    title: 'The Mid-Autumn Festival',
    passage: 'The Mid-Autumn Festival is one of the most important traditional festivals in China. It is celebrated on the fifteenth day of the eighth month of the Chinese lunar calendar, when the moon is believed to be the brightest and roundest of the whole year. The festival has a history of more than three thousand years, and it is a time for families to come together. On this evening, people usually enjoy a big dinner with their grandparents, parents, and other relatives. After dinner, families sit outside or by a window and admire the full moon while eating mooncakes and pomelos. Mooncakes are round pastries with sweet fillings such as red bean paste, lotus seed paste, or egg yolk. They symbolise reunion and happiness, and people often give boxes of mooncakes as gifts to friends and colleagues. In some parts of China, people also light colourful lanterns, and children carry small rabbit-shaped lanterns through the streets. There is a beautiful old story about Chang\'e, a kind woman who flew to the moon and lived there with a jade rabbit. The festival reminds people that no matter how far apart family members live, they can always look at the same moon and feel close to each other. For me, the festival is not only about delicious food, but also about love and togetherness.',
