    id: 'read-lv2-40',
    level: 2,
    serial: 40,
    category: '科学',
    title: 'The Water Cycle',
    passage: 'Water is everywhere around us, in oceans, rivers, lakes, clouds, and even in the air we breathe. But the water we use today is the same water that has been moving around the earth for billions of years. This movement is called the water cycle, and it never stops. The cycle has four main stages. First, the sun heats up water in rivers, lakes, and oceans. Some of this water turns into water vapour, a gas that rises into the air. This step is called evaporation. As the vapour goes higher, the air becomes colder, and the vapour changes back into tiny drops of water. These drops join together to form clouds, and this step is called condensation. When the clouds become too heavy, the water falls back to the ground as rain or snow. This is called precipitation. Some of the rain soaks into the soil and becomes groundwater, while the rest flows into rivers and eventually returns to the sea. Then the whole cycle begins again. The water cycle is very important because it brings fresh water to plants, animals, and people. Without it, life on earth would not be possible. That is why we should never waste water, and we should keep our rivers and oceans clean.',
