    id: 'read-lv2-44',
    level: 2,
    serial: 44,
    category: '历史',
    title: 'The Ancient Silk Road',
    passage: 'More than two thousand years ago, during the Han Dynasty, a brave Chinese traveller named Zhang Qian opened a long route that would change history. He travelled west from the capital Chang\'an, across deserts and high mountains, looking for new trading partners. His journey led to the creation of the Silk Road, a network of land and sea routes that connected China with Central Asia, the Middle East, Europe, and even parts of Africa. Along these routes, merchants carried silk, tea, porcelain, and spices from China, and brought back gold, silver, horses, glassware, and rare stones from other lands. The Silk Road was not just about goods. Buddhist monks travelled along it to bring new religious ideas to China, while artists, musicians, and scientists shared their knowledge with each other. Towns such as Dunhuang, Kashgar, and Samarkand grew rich and famous because they lay at important crossroads. Caravans of camels carried heavy loads across the burning Gobi desert and the snowy Pamir mountains. Travelling was slow and dangerous, and many traders never returned home. Today, parts of the ancient Silk Road are protected as World Heritage sites, and a modern "Belt and Road" project aims to build new roads, railways, and bridges to connect countries once again. The Silk Road reminds us that human beings have always reached out to one another.',
