    title: 'Tu Youyou and the Cure for Malaria',
    passage: 'Tu Youyou is a Chinese scientist whose discovery has saved millions of lives around the world. She was born in 1930 in Ningbo, Zhejiang Province. From a young age, she was curious about nature and liked reading science books. She later studied medicine at Peking University and became a researcher at the China Academy of Traditional Chinese Medicine. In the 1960s and 1970s, malaria was a deadly disease spreading across many countries in Asia and Africa. Common medicines were no longer working, and many patients were dying. Tu Youyou was given the job of finding a new treatment. Instead of looking only at modern chemistry, she studied old Chinese medical texts and noticed that a plant called sweet wormwood had been used for hundreds of years to treat fevers. After many careful experiments, she and her team found a way to extract a special substance from the plant. They named it artemisinin, and it worked amazingly well against malaria. In 2015, Tu Youyou became the first Chinese woman to win the Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine. She donated part of her prize money to support young scientists. Her story shows that traditional knowledge and modern science can work together to change the world.',
