    title: 'The Art of Chinese Tea',
    passage: 'Tea has been part of Chinese life for thousands of years. According to legend, the Emperor Shennong discovered tea by accident in 2737 BC, when some tea leaves fell into his pot of hot water. From that day on, drinking tea became a daily habit for people across China. China is the birthplace of tea, and there are many famous kinds of Chinese tea, including green tea, black tea, oolong tea, white tea, and pu\'er tea. Each type has its own taste, colour, and health benefits. Tea is not only a drink; it is also a symbol of Chinese culture and hospitality. When guests visit a Chinese home, they are usually offered a cup of hot tea before anything else. In traditional Chinese tea ceremonies, the host prepares tea slowly and carefully, using small clay teapots, fair cups, and tiny tea cups. Every movement is calm and graceful, and the aim is to bring peace to the mind and respect between people. In some southern cities, people gather in old tea houses to drink tea, play chess, and chat for hours. Today, Chinese tea is enjoyed in almost every country in the world. However, no matter how popular it becomes, it still carries the quiet, gentle spirit of Chinese tradition.',
