    category: '环境',
    title: 'Recycling at Home and School',
    passage: 'Every year, people around the world produce billions of tonnes of rubbish. Much of this rubbish comes from homes and schools, including paper, plastic bottles, food waste, and old clothes. If we simply throw everything into one big bin, it will end up in landfills or oceans, where it can stay for hundreds of years and harm wildlife. Recycling is a simple but powerful way to reduce this problem. By sorting waste into different groups, we can give many materials a second life. Paper can be recycled into new notebooks and cardboard boxes. Glass bottles can be melted and made into new containers. Metal cans can be turned into bicycles or parts for cars. Even food waste and garden leaves can be turned into natural compost to help plants grow. At my school, we have four different coloured bins in every classroom: blue for paper, green for glass, red for plastic, and yellow for other rubbish. Every Friday, our class monitor checks the bins and leads a short talk on how to sort waste correctly. At home, my family keeps a small recycling bag in the kitchen, and we rinse bottles and cans before putting them out. Small actions like these, done by millions of families, can really make our planet cleaner and greener.',
