    serial: 48,
    category: '科学',
    title: 'How Plants Make Food',
    passage: 'Have you ever wondered how a tiny green plant can grow into a tall tree without eating any food like we do? The answer lies in a special process called photosynthesis. Almost all green plants can make their own food using just sunlight, water, and a gas called carbon dioxide from the air. Photosynthesis mainly happens inside the leaves. Inside each leaf, there is a green colouring material called chlorophyll, which gives plants their green colour. Chlorophyll is very good at catching sunlight. When sunlight shines on a leaf, the chlorophyll uses its energy to combine carbon dioxide, taken in from tiny holes in the leaf, with water that the roots have sucked up from the soil. Together, they produce a kind of sugar, which is the plant\'s food. As a side product, oxygen is released into the air through the same tiny holes. This oxygen is what we and other animals need to breathe. Photosynthesis is therefore essential for almost all life on earth. Without it, there would be no food, no oxygen, and no animals or humans. So the next time you see a green leaf dancing in the sunlight, remember that it is quietly making food and giving us life at the same time.',
